Project Overview:

In this project, a Campus Event Booking System is designed based on Java
and object oriented programming principles and a graphical user interface based
on the JavaFX. The system simulates the life cycle of campus events and
reservations, enabling the administrators to handle users, create and maintain events,
accept and process bookings, impose booking constraints, and handle waitlists.

Phase 1 deployed the fundamental system architecture comprising of user hierarchy,
event hierarchy, booking workflow and waitlist logic. Phase 2 builds upon the system
to a full application with CSV file loading, graphical interface navigation between
modules, waitlist promotion notifications, search and filtering capability, and test support.

Starter datasets are loaded automatically when the application is started to ensure the system
starts in a realistic predefined state instead of having to enter the data manually.

System Architecture:

The design in this project is a Campus Event Booking System that is developed using Java
and object oriented programming concepts and JavaFX developing graphical user interface.
The system is a simulated life cycle of the campus events and reservations, and it allows
the administrators to manage users, create events, maintain events, accept and process
bookings, impose booking constraints and manage waitlists.

Phase 1 deployed the fundamental system architecture comprising of user hierarchy,
event hierarchy, booking workflow and waitlist logic. Phase 2 builds upon the system
to a full application with CSV file loading, graphical interface navigation between modules,
waitlist promotion notifications, search and filtering capability, and test support.

To get the system into a realistic predefined state, starter datasets are loaded automatically when the
application starts rather than forcing the user to enter the data himself.

CSV Data Loading:

When the system is started, it reads three input files:

users.csv
events.csv
bookings.csv

These files are read by the FileLoader which automatically recreates all system objects.
Using the booking status and timestamp values in the dataset, confirmed bookings,
waitlisted bookings, cancelled bookings and event rosters are restored.

The createdAt timestamp is used to rebuild waitlists to keep it first come first served.

This enables the application to start running with an already initialized dataset.

Modules and Features:

- User Management
All system users are recorded in the User Management module. There are three types
of users that are supported by the system including Student, Staff, and Guest.

A user has a distinct userId, name, email and user type.

The module enables an administrator to add users, avoid duplication of userids,
access a summary of individual user information, show a summary of confirmed
bookings, and the list of all users in readable format.

- Event Management
The Event Management module deals with the development and maintenance of
campus events. The system is compatible with Seminar, Workshop and Concert event types.

Each event has an eventId, title, date and time, location, capacity, status and a type specific field.

A topic field is stored in workshop events. Store Name of a speaker in seminar events.
An informational ageRestriction field is stored in the concert events.

Administrators are able to add events, edit event details, cancel events, view list of events, search
events by title, filter events by type, as well as, view event roster with confirmed bookings and waitlists.

In the case of an event being canceled, all the confirmed and waitlisted bookings get cancelled
automatically and the waitlist gets removed.

- Booking Management
The Booking Management module links users and events and implements booking regulations and capacity constraints.

Every booking has a bookingId, userid, eventid, createdAt time, and booking status.

The booking regulations make sure that a user is not allowed to book an event twice.
When there is space in the event the booking is confirmed. In case the event is already
booked, then it is waitlisted. When a confirmed booking is cancelled the first waitlisted
booking is automatically promoted.

A user type has varying booking limits. Students can have three confirmed bookings.
Employees are allowed to make five reservations. Visitors are allowed to make one reservation.

The module enables the administrators to make bookings, cancel bookings, see bookings made by the user
and see event rosters divided into confirmed and waitlisted.

When the promotion takes place, automatic waitlist promotion messages are shown in the user interface.

- Waitlist Management
The Waitlist Management module maintains the ordering and integrity of waitlists for each event.

When events reach capacity additional bookings are placed on the waitlist in timestamp order. The
system ensures waitlists follow a first come first served structure and prevents duplicate waitlist entries.

Administrators can view event waitlists including user details and timestamps,
remove waitlisted bookings, and observe promotion notifications when waitlisted users become confirmed.

When an event is cancelled the waitlist is cleared automatically.

- User Interface Design
The graphical interface has a navigation panel which will offer access to User Management, Event Management,
Booking Management and Waitlist Management modules.

All the modules provide the information in the form of structured lists and tables.
Forms enable the administrators to create users, create events, and create bookings.
Status updates can be shown as soon as the booking changes are made.

Waitlist Management panel shows waitlist position, user details, booking time, and event summary details. Promotion
messages are shown when they are moved off the waitlist into the confirmed section.

- Data Persistence Support
On application startup, the system reads starter data in CSV files using the FileLoader class. Users, events,
bookings, waitlists and booking status values are automatically restored by these files.

The architecture aids in the reconstruction of the full system state among
program executions and guarantees that the order of waitlists is maintained through
the reconstruction with the help of timestamps.

- Testing and Validation
The project has a list of test scenarios that authenticate booking workflow logic.

Examples of test cases are checking bookings when the capacity is available,
bookings on a waitlist when events are full, promoting waitlisted bookings following
cancellations, and duplicate bookings.

The tests are certain that booking rules and waitlist behavior are working properly.

- Object Oriented Programming Concepts Demonstrated
This system is encapsulated in terms of private attributes and getter setter methods.
Implementation of inheritance is based on Student, Staff and Guest subclasses and Workshop,
Seminar and Concert subclasses. Base User and Event classes are used to describe abstraction.
Polymorphism enables base class references to be used to deal with users and events.

ArrayList and Queue collections are utilized to handle users, events, bookings, and waitlists.

-GitHub Submission Requirements
The repository of the project has incremental commits of the development showing
it is progressing towards the two phases of the project. The repository contains at least ten
commits which have descriptive commit messages detailing the development efforts.

Examples of commit messages are adding the event hierarchy, adding booking service logic,
adding waitlist promotion functionality, adding CSV loading, and adding Waitlist Management interface.

Before submission all group members and the teaching assistant entered the repository
and the latter was entered as a collaborator.

- How to Run the Program
Launch the project in IntelliJ IDEA or any other Java IDE. Have all source files contained within the src directory. Execute the file
in the UI package, MainUI.java.

The system loads users.csv, events.csv and bookings.csv as the application starts and restores the system state.

The navigation panel will allow you to access user management, event management, booking management, and waitlist management features.

- Assumptions
The system is administrator operated. CSV datasets can be started at runtime. The values of age restriction on
concerts are not binding, but only informational and are not implemented as a rule in booking. During execution,
bookings are held in memory. Booking timestamps are used to determine waitlist ordering. The graphical interface
displays the updates on booking status as soon as they have been changed.










